export const lsConcepts9011000: any[] = [
  { id: "ls-nama-901", title: "Nādarūpiṇī", category: "Dharma-tattva", summary: "She who is in the form of the primal sound. Literally nāda-rūpiṇī means She whose form is nāda, the unstruck subtle resonance that precedes audible speech and marks the first tremor of Śiva-Śakti toward manifestation. Doctrinally the passage treats Her as Śabda-Brahman itself: where designator and designated are still one, OM embodies this vibration, and the whole world is said to emerge from nāda-bindu, echoed by the Muṇḍaka teaching that the imperishable source births the universe. Only Śakti, who carries this sound in the central channel, can therefore reveal Śiva. In practice, withdraw from outer noise, visualize a luminous vibration pulsing gently in the heart, and rest attention there until thought dissolves into listening.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-167"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-902", title: "Vijñānakalanā", category: "Dharma-tattva", summary: "She who realizes the knowledge of brahman. Vijñāna here means ripened liberating knowledge rather than information, and kalanā means She effects or brings it about, continuing Her role as giver of Śiva-knowledge. The passage frames mastery of the fourteen branches of learning, the four Vedas with their six auxiliaries plus reasoning, inquiry, and sacred history, as the fullness called vijñāna, which She alone bestows and awakens. Thus study without grace remains accumulation, while grace turns learning into realization. In practice, dedicate daily study and reflection to Her, then sit in silence afterward so that concepts settle into direct recognition rather than pride of scholarship.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-167"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-903", title: "Kalyā", category: "Dharma-tattva", summary: "She who is capable of creation. Kalyā carries senses of auspicious, hale, skillful, ready at dawn, and instructive, gathering readiness, health, and blessedness into one epithet. The passage also links Her to mastery of the arts and to embodiment of the kalās, the subtle powers through which creativity flows, so creation here is both cosmic making and refined artistry. She is fertile power made gracious, able to begin, shape, and bless. In practice, begin any creative work at a fresh hour with a brief salutation to Her, asking that skill serve auspiciousness rather than ego. The commentary further stresses that auspicious readiness is itself a power: because She is prepared, the worlds can begin, and because She is skillful in sacred arts, beginnings become beautiful. Meditate at daybreak on Her as the alert Mother, asking that your own plans start in clarity and ripen into lasting good.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-167"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-904", title: "Vidagdhā", category: "Dharma-tattva", summary: "She who is expert in everything. Vidagdhā means clever, discerning, artful, and resourceful, able to devise means suited to every situation. The passage applies this ingenuity to the triple cosmic work of emergence, maintenance, and dissolution: such vast administration requires perfect intelligence, timing, and adaptability, all affirmed as Hers. Her cleverness is thus a Brahmanic perfection, not worldly cunning, the wisdom that never misfires. In practice, when facing a tangled problem, pause, acknowledge the limits of personal shrewdness, and invoke Her clear ingenuity before choosing the next skillful step. The passage insists this mastery spans making, maintaining, and withdrawing the worlds, so no detail escapes Her discernment and no emergency outwits Her response. Such all-competence confirms Her identity with the all-knowing ground. When deliberating, name the alternatives calmly before Her, then choose the course that best serves truth rather than vanity.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-167"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-905", title: "Baindavāsanā", category: "Dharma-tattva", summary: "She who is seated in the baindava (spot between the eyebrows) chakra. Baindava-āsanā means enthroned upon the bindu, the central point of Śrīcakra and also the meeting of the three channels in the brow center. The passage unfolds the kāmakalā teaching of white, red, and mingled drops symbolizing Śiva, Śakti, and their union, whose expansion and contraction generate the world, while their meeting in the practitioner lifts individual awareness into universality. She sits exactly where duality gathers into a point. In practice, gently gather breath and attention to the brow point, sense Her seated there as still radiance, and let the sense of separate seer soften.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-167"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-906", title: "Tattvādhikā", category: "Dharma-tattva", summary: "She who transcends all cosmic categories. Tattva means a constitutive principle, whether counted as twenty-four or thirty-six, and adhikā means beyond or surpassing them. The passage presents Her as Brahman, which no enumeration can contain: principles describe prakṛti's unfolding, but She as the Absolute stands free of the very map She authorizes. This transcendence guarantees that no cosmology exhausts Her. In practice, use lists of principles as ladders for understanding, then consciously step beyond them into silent witnessing of the One who exceeds every category. The passage appeals to the standard enumerations of principles, whether two dozen or three dozen, only to declare Her their witness and origin rather than their item. This protects the seeker from mistaking the map for the territory. Study cosmologies respectfully, then release them in silent adoration of the One who both authors and exceeds every scheme.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-168"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-907", title: "Tattvamayī", category: "Dharma-tattva", summary: "She who is reality itself; she who is shiva Himself. Tattva-mayī means composed of, pervaded by, and embodying the ultimate principles, complementing the previous name's transcendence. The passage surveys tattvas from gross elements through inner instruments to pure wisdom principles culminating in Śakti and Śiva, declaring Her both the all-pervading Brahman and the prakṛti whose evolutes these principles are. As Brahman She exceeds tattvas, as Mother She becomes them. In practice, honor every element of body, sense, and mind as Her substance, caring for them reverently while remembering the Whole they express. The passage details outer instruments from ether to earth with senses and faculties, inner instruments of mind, intellect, awareness, and ego, further wisdom principles, and finally pure power and pure witnessing, declaring Her the soul of the entire ladder. Nothing real stands outside Her. Treat body, breath, and thought as Her textures, purifying them as one tends a temple.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-168"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-908", title: "Tattvamarthasvarūpiṇī", category: "Dharma-tattva", summary: "She who is the meaning of tat (that) and tvam (thou). The compound points to the great sentence Thou art That, where tat names Brahman and tvam names the innermost self. Doctrinally She is the living sense of that identity: without Her grace the formula remains grammar, while through Her it becomes recognition that the subtlest essence expounded in the Chāndogya is one's own Self. She bridges scripture and experience. In practice, repeat the great sentence slowly, first reflecting on its words, then releasing words into wordless self-presence under Her guidance. The passage unfolds the Upanishadic assurance that the subtlest essence is the Self, truth itself, and that recognition of this identity is liberation. Her grace turns sentence into sight. Take one phrase of the great teaching each morning, reflect on its two terms, then rest wordlessly in the unity they indicate under Her gaze.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-168"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-909", title: "Sāmagānapriyā", category: "Dharma-tattva", summary: "She who is fond of the chanting of the sāma veda. Sāma-gāna means the Rig verses lifted into melody, and priyā means She loves both the song and its singers. The passage recalls Śiva's delight in Sāman chant, Rāvaṇa's propitiation through it, Kṛṣṇa's claim to be Sāman among Vedas, and the Upaniṣadic image of earth as ṛc and fire as sāman joined into song. Melody thus becomes theology: truth sung with devotion melts resistance. In practice, chant or listen to sacred melody with feeling rather than haste, offering tone and attention together as love made audible. The passage explains the etymology of song from earth and fire joined, and honors the moon-rite and forest teachings rooted in sung Veda, showing melody as a vehicle of metaphysics rather than mere ornament. Sound sweetened by devotion carries meaning deeper. Chant a single sacred verse melodiously each day, letting tone soften the heart so understanding can enter.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-168"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-910", title: "Saumyā", category: "Dharma-tattva", summary: "She who is benign and gentle in nature; of a cool, gentle nature as the moon. Saumyā evokes moonlike calm, auspiciousness, cheer, and approachability, while soma also hints at Śiva united with Umā and at the sacred soma offering. The passage holds together Her tenderness, Her worthiness of solemn worship, and Her power to cool fevered minds, showing gentleness as strength rather than weakness. She soothes without indulging. In practice, when agitation rises, turn the face inward toward Her moonlike gentleness, breathe slowly, and let Her coolness settle thought before action. The passage notes the sacrificial reading as well: She is worthy of the solemn soma offering, while Her lunar coolness calms inner fever and Her cheer restores hope. Auspiciousness here is therapeutic. When the mind burns with urgency or resentment, pause for slow breaths, picture moonlight on still water, and ask Her gentleness to temper your speech.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-168"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-911", title: "Sadāśivakuṭumbinī", category: "Dharma-tattva", summary: "She who is the wife of sadāśiva. Kuṭumbinī means belonging to and sustaining the household, here the family of Sadāśiva, the luminous principle where the universe is felt as one's own limbs in undivided I-awareness. The passage places Sadāśiva near the summit of the pure principles, above will-wisdom and lordship and below only Śakti and Śiva, marking Her as intimate partner of the almost-ultimate. Union, not solitude, crowns realization. In practice, contemplate companionate divinity rather than isolated absolutes, bringing household love itself into worship as a mirror of cosmic belonging. The passage outlines the ascending pure principles culminating in undifferentiated I-am-this awareness, where the universe is experienced as limbs of one's own consciousness. Her household is therefore the almost-absolute itself. Reflect on family bonds as training for cosmic belonging, serving those near you as though serving the limbs of that vast household.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-168"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-912", title: "Savyāpasavyamārgasthā", category: "Dharma-tattva", summary: "She who occupies (or can be reached by) both the left and right paths of worship. Savya means the right-hand Vedic way and apasavya the left-hand Tantric way, with mārga as path. The passage contrasts inclusive worship of many sustaining powers with exclusive devotion to one chosen deity, warning that sectarian narrowness can delay the vision of the Whole, while affirming that She meets practitioners on either road and also governs the soul's bright and shadowed postmortem passages. No sincere approach misses Her. In practice, honor your own discipline without despising others, and keep steady ethical and contemplative effort so that whichever road you walk leads upward.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-169"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-913", title: "Sarvāpadvinivāriṇī", category: "Dharma-tattva", summary: "She who removes all dangers. Sarva-āpad-vinivāriṇī means She turns aside every misfortune, outer and inner. The passage connects calamity with abandoning both illuminated paths, citing Upaniṣadic warnings about falling into aimless rebirth, and preserves the Goddess's promise that devoted remembrance, mantra, sacred names, compassionate conduct, and meditation on Her feet consume even mountainous adversity like a lamp lit in darkness. Remembrance is protection. In practice, at the first sign of trouble, steady the mind, recall Her feet with trust, and resume right conduct instead of panic. The passage preserves Her direct assurance that refuge, compassionate conduct, mantra, and holy names consume towering misfortune like a lamp kindled in darkness, while reminding that mental transformation matters more than mere ritual. Devotion must ripen into knowing. Keep a brief daily remembrance of Her feet, especially when plans falter, and align action with kindness.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-169"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-914", title: "Svasthā", category: "Dharma-tattva", summary: "She who abides in Herself; She who is free from all afflictions. Svasthā means established in one's own natural state, healthy and whole without imposed conditions. The passage draws on the dialogue about where the Infinite rests, upon its own power alone, and on the insight that pleasure and pain enter only with a sense of secondness, illustrated by the tale of the child who wounds a creature and learns the Mother feels it. As the highest Self beyond whom nothing stands, She is effortlessly Self-poised. In practice, rest attention in simple self-presence without grasping another object, and treat every being as Her limb.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-169"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-915", title: "Svabhāvamadhurā", category: "Dharma-tattva", summary: "She who is sweet in Her inherent nature. Svabhāva-madhurā means sweetness belongs to Her essence, not to mood or strategy, so all are naturally drawn to Her. The passage plays on syllables to show Her bearing the yoke of the wise, illumining their road, chiefly imparting Self-knowledge, and dwelling pleasantly within devotees, concluding that She is realized within through knowing love. Her charm is salvific pedagogy. In practice, cultivate inner sweetness through kindness and devotional remembrance, letting your own nature grow honeyed enough to reflect Hers. The passage breaks the name into syllables of shining, wisdom-bearing, and chiefly guiding, portraying Her as the radiant yoke that lights the sages' road and as the supreme teacher of Self-knowledge dwelling sweetly within devotees. Attraction to Her is the beginning of wisdom. Sweeten speech and motive today, observing how inner honey draws others toward peace.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-169"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-916", title: "Dhīrā", category: "Dharma-tattva", summary: "She who is wise; She who gives wisdom. Dhīrā means steady, resolute intelligence, the strength that comes from seeing truly. The passage identifies this strength as nondual understanding itself: firmness of mind born when multiplicity is recognized as one ground, a realization granted only through Her. Wisdom is therefore Her gift and Her nature at once. In practice, when distracted by opposing appearances, return to the single awareness holding them, asking Her to steady insight until it becomes unshakable. The passage calls this steadiness the strength of nonduality itself, attained only through Her, since fragmented seeing breeds fear while unified seeing breeds courage. Intellect becomes fortitude when it rests in oneness. In difficulty, steady the breath, affirm the single ground beneath opposites, and let Her firmness replace anxious wavering with quiet resolve.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-169"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-917", title: "Dhīrasamarcitā", category: "Dharma-tattva", summary: "She who is worshipped by the wise. Dhīra-samarcitā means the steady knowers honor Her, since those who seek ultimate understanding naturally turn to its source. The passage unfolds dhī as non-separation from the essence of knowing, rasa as fullness of joy, and worship as the means by which knowledge ripens into bliss. Adoration completes inquiry. In practice, join study with reverence: learn, then offer what was learned back in gratitude, letting understanding flower into devotion. The passage unpacks knowledge as non-separation from essence, fullness as plenitude of joy, and worship as their meeting, so that scholars praise Her because inquiry itself leads to Her threshold. Learning fulfilled becomes love. After study, close books briefly, bow inwardly to the source of insight, and dedicate understanding to the welfare of all.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-169"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-918", title: "Caitanyārghyasamārādhyā", category: "Dharma-tattva", summary: "She who is worshipped with consciousness as the oblation. Caitanya means free foundational awareness, and arghya the respectful water offered to a guest, so the name enjoins offering awareness itself. The passage cites the equation of Self with consciousness and the Tantric maxim that knowledge is the true libation, adding that devotion wedded to understanding liberates, while mere outer flowers do not. The highest hospitality is attention. In practice, pour collected conscious presence at Her feet in meditation, making each moment of clear noticing an oblation. The passage recalls aided teachings on pure awareness manifesting as ruler, womb, and radiance, and on conditioned consciousness in object, knower, and instrument, urging the offering of the fourth, unconditioned knowing. Attention is the costliest gift. Practice presenting bare awareness itself, watching thoughts arise and pass without grasping, as flowers laid at Her feet.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-170"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-919", title: "Caitanyakusumapriyā", category: "Dharma-tattva", summary: "She who is fond of the flower that is consciousness. Kusuma-priyā means lover of blossoms, here the eightfold blossom of virtues: non-harm, sense restraint, compassion to beings, forbearance, wisdom, austerity, meditation, and truthfulness. The passage insists She desires these inner flowers over plucked petals, recalling the Gītā's acceptance of leaf, flower, fruit, or water offered with love and the Sudāmā story where humble devotion outweighs grandeur. Character is bouquet. In practice, lay one virtue daily before Her in action, letting conduct become the garland She wears. The passage verses the eight blossoms in full and adds the tale of humble parched rice transformed by love into abundance, proving She weighs feeling above grandeur. Inner virtues outshine outer gardens. Each evening examine which of the eight flowers wilted today, resolve to water it tomorrow, and offer the whole garland mentally before sleep.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-170"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-920", title: "Sadoditā", category: "Dharma-tattva", summary: "She who is ever shining. Sadā-uditā means perpetually risen, like a sun that never sets in the world of Brahman. The passage links Her to solar imagery used throughout the hymn and to the Upaniṣadic realm where daylight neither comes nor goes, adding that She dawns continually in virtuous minds. Her radiance is constancy itself. In practice, recall Her during both elation and dullness, trusting that the inner sun persists even when clouds of mood obscure it. The passage compares Her to solar splendor sung earlier in the hymn and to the undecaying light of the Brahma-world, adding that She dawns in purified minds without interruption. Constancy, not intensity, is Her signature. Establish one fixed moment of remembrance at sunrise or dusk, keeping it faithfully so Her steadiness shapes your rhythm.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-170"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-921", title: "Sadātuṣṭā", category: "Dharma-tattva", summary: "She who is ever pleased. Sadā-tuṣṭā means abiding contentment untouched by want. The passage explains this as the natural poise of Brahman, which acts through no need and witnesses all without agitation, and notes that true devotion shares this sufficiency. Realizing the ever-content, the seeker becomes content. In practice, meet outcomes with equanimity, offer effort without clutching results, and learn satisfaction from Her example. The passage roots Her satisfaction in need-free witnessing: because Brahman partakes in no compulsive doing, discontent finds no footing, and devotees who realize this share the same sufficiency. Contentment is contagious. Practice thanking Her for what already sustains you before petitioning for more, letting gratitude mature into unshakable serenity.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-170"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-922", title: "Taruṇādityapāṭalā", category: "Dharma-tattva", summary: "She who is rosy like the morning sun. Taruṇa-āditya-pāṭalā pictures the pale-red glow of sunrise, blending Her red predominance in sustaining the world with a touch of Śiva's whiteness. The passage surveys scriptural color symbolism, where different hues aid different aspirations, and notes that cakra meditation likewise associates colors with qualities, yet Her rosy dawn remains central. Color teaches function. In practice, visualize Her sunrise hue when seeking vitality and courage, and Her moon-white calm when seeking clarity and peace. The passage surveys scriptural hues from turmeric yellow to lotus white and lightning flash, teaching that devotees may contemplate Her in colors suited to their need, courage in red, purity in white, while the subtle centers likewise glow with functional colors. Form adapts to aspiration. Choose the hue matching today's task and visualize it softly at the heart.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-170"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-923", title: "Dakṣiṇādakṣiṇārādhyā", category: "Dharma-tattva", summary: "She who is adored by both right and left-handed worshippers. The doubled dakṣiṇā embraces both Vedic and Tantric devotees, and by extension both the learned who seek liberation without bargaining and the simple who still pray for favors. The passage adds that dakṣiṇā also means the offering to the teacher, so honoring the guru who transmits Śrīvidyā pleases Her, recalling Kṛṣṇa's grading of devotees with special love for the wise. Breadth does not dilute depth. In practice, respect every sincere worshiper, support your teachers generously, and purify motive from bargaining toward liberation. The passage distinguishes petitionary ritual from liberating wisdom, counting four classes of devotees with the sage-lover dearest, and interprets offerings to the preceptor as offerings to Her who transmits the lore. Motive determines altitude. Examine why you pray, gradually shifting requests from possession and relief toward knowledge and freedom, while honoring teachers materially and sincerely.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-171"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-924", title: "Darasmeramukhāmbujā", category: "Dharma-tattva", summary: "She whose lotus face holds a sweet smile. Dara-smera means gently blossoming, describing a face like a lotus opening and a smile born of rejoicing in the Self. The passage gathers earlier praises of that smile, compares teeth to conch and neck-lines to sacred syllables, and stresses that even amid cosmic dissolution, while Śiva dances the worlds to rest, She remains a smiling witness, fearless because established within. Bliss is composure. In practice, recall Her smiling lotus-face when fear arises, breathing with the thought that one anchored in the Self need not flinch. The passage compares teeth to gleaming shell, neck-lines to primal syllables, and whole presence to blossoming lotus, noting even the Destroyer's glance cannot dim Her dawn-like face. Fearlessness flowers as a smile. When anxiety tightens the face, consciously relax brow and lips while remembering Her, letting outer softening invite inner courage.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-171"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-925", title: "Kaulinī kevalā", category: "Dharma-tattva", summary: "She who is worshipped as pure knowledge (consciousness) by the spiritual aspirants following the kaula path. Kaulinī names the Goddess of the Kaula way and kevalā the pure unified state beyond pleasure and pain where opposites fall silent. The passage teaches that the limited practitioner, contacting the Self's power, is raised into equality with it, so Kaula worshipers are transformed into kevalins through Her. Method matures into identity. In practice, follow your discipline sincerely while releasing fascination with opposites, aiming at the quiet unity where worshiper and worshiped coincide. The passage cites the aphorism on freedom from pleasure and pain and the vibration teaching that the bound individual, touching the Self's power, rises into equality with it, so that left-path devotees mature into pure ones through Her aid. Practice ripens into identity. Persist in discipline without fetishizing method, aiming always at the silent unity beyond duality.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-171"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-926", title: "Anarghyakaivalyapadadāyinī", category: "Dharma-tattva", summary: "She who confers the priceless fruit of final liberation. Anarghya means beyond price, kaivalya the isolation of spirit in its essence free from transmigration, and padadāyinī the giver of that state. The passage distinguishes soul from nature, describes discriminative perfection ripening in desireless absorption, and cites Patañjali that freedom is the being's own nature awaiting discovery, attainable only with Her aid. Grace unlocks what effort prepares. In practice, combine ethical clarity, yogic stillness, and prayer for Her help, trusting liberation as your buried nature, not a foreign prize. The passage elaborates isolation as freedom even amid subtle realms, perfected in desireless absorption where discriminative knowing culminates, already embedded as the soul's nature awaiting yogic uncovering. Effort reveals, grace completes. Combine moral clarity, steady posture, measured breath, and prayer, trusting the buried freedom to surface like a forgotten treasure.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-171"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-927", title: "Stotrapriyā", category: "Dharma-tattva", summary: "She who is fond of hymns in Her praise. Stotra means verses meant to be sung, distinguished from treatises meant to be recited, and traditionally covering salutation, blessing, celebration of deeds, glory, and prayer. The passage notes the thousand names themselves embody these modes and commends beloved hymns and Vedic suktas as dear to Her, mentioning rarer protective litanies as well. Sung praise tunes the heart. In practice, sing or chant Her glories regularly with feeling, letting melody carry attention from distraction into devotion. The passage classifies praise into salutation, blessing, celebration of deeds, heroism, glory, and petition, finding all six woven through the thousand names, and commends both temple litanies and rare forest hymns for removing adversity. Singing orders the soul. Keep a small hymnal practice, voicing praise aloud when possible and inwardly when not, until the heart grows fluent in gratitude.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-172"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-928", title: "Stutimatī", category: "Dharma-tattva", summary: "She who is the true object, the essence, of all praises. Stuti-matī can mean She possesses the wisdom praised by hymns, and also that praised, one gains both insight and abundance. The passage teaches that hymns sung in the previous name mature here into realization: eulogy becomes epistemology, turning admiration into liberating understanding and support for life. Praise bears fruit. In practice, after chanting, sit briefly in silence to receive clarity and steadiness, dedicating both wisdom and welfare back to their source. The passage plays on knowledge joined to abundance, promising that sincere eulogy yields both liberating insight and sustaining welfare. Worship integrates heaven and earth. After chanting, briefly intend the welfare of loved ones as well as your own illumination, letting praise overflow from self toward world.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-172"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-929", title: "Śrutisaṃstutavaibhavā", category: "Dharma-tattva", summary: "She whose glory is celebrated in the shrutis. Śruti means the heard revelation, and saṃstuta-vaibhavā means whose majesty is collectively hymned there. The passage appeals to the Bahvṛca Upaniṣad's vision of the Goddess as creatrix and Brahman, recalls Her intrinsic powers of peace, knowledge, stability, and restraint through which even Śiva enjoys bliss, and distinguishes eternal hearing from remembered tradition. Scripture confirms experience. In practice, study Upaniṣadic passages on the Goddess contemplatively, then test them in meditation so that text and life illumine each other. The passage recalls peace, knowledge, stability, and restraint as Her intrinsic powers through which even the still Lord enjoys bliss, grounding Vedic celebration in lived plenitude and distinguishing eternally heard truth from merely remembered lore. Glory has substance. Dedicate weekly time to scriptural recitation followed by quiet sitting, so hearing matures into beholding.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-172"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-930", title: "Manasvinī", category: "Dharma-tattva", summary: "She who is well-known for Her mind. Manasvinī praises a noble, independent, self-possessed mind, free will poised before manifestation has begun. The passage calls this essential independence Her nature, steady equanimity untouched by compulsion, and recalls the Upaniṣadic counsel that Brahman is to be realized through mind alone. Sovereignty of awareness is Hers. In practice, train the mind toward steadiness rather than reactivity, offering scattered thoughts back into one clear intention fixed on Her. The passage names this self-dependence the unstarted will poised in transcendence before the willed has emerged, the very freedom of the Absolute conscious of its own nature. Equanimity is sovereignty. Guard one daily interval of chosen stillness, refusing compulsive reaction, to taste the self-rule She exemplifies.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-172"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-931", title: "Mānavatī", category: "Dharma-tattva", summary: "She who is high-minded; She who has great fame. Mānavatī means elevated in feeling and honor, exalted in style and magnanimity. The passage reads this nobility as forgiveness and compassion capacious enough to mother the worlds, the very greatness that earns Her the title of Divine Mother. Dignity here is mercy. In practice, answer injury with forbearance and meanness with generosity, letting high-mindedness make your conduct worthy of Her name. The passage finds in this elevation the forgiveness and compassion that make Her the Divine Mother, greatness measured by mercy rather than dominance. Nobility nurtures. Today elevate one interaction: listen longer, excuse faster, and speak of others' potential rather than their faults, as Her high-minded child.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-172"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-932", title: "Maheśī", category: "Dharma-tattva", summary: "She who is the wife of mahesha (shiva). Maheśī is the consort of the Great Lord, also hailed elsewhere as Māheśvarī, sharing His sovereignty while springing from Him. The passage treats the title as affirming both intimacy and origin: She belongs to none lesser, and Her power is His power in feminine form. Partnership expresses ultimacy. In practice, honor the divine couple together, harmonizing stillness and energy, reverence and love, in your own inner life. The passage notes twin later titles echoing the same queenship and hints that She springs from the Great Lord even as She weds Him, paradox of power proceeding from stillness to move worlds. Origin and union coincide. Contemplate any creative partnership in your life, balancing receptivity with resolve as an icon of Their concord.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-172"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-933", title: "Maṅgalākṛtiḥ", category: "Dharma-tattva", summary: "She who is of auspicious form. Maṅgala means felicity, welfare, and bliss, and ākṛti means embodied shape, so every line of Her is goodness made visible. The passage reaffirms earlier teachings on auspiciousness and notes that Her very sight confers benefit. Beauty is benediction. In practice, begin undertakings with Her auspicious glance in mind, and shape surroundings, speech, and dress toward cleanliness and kindness that invite welfare. The passage reaffirms earlier teachings on benevolent embodiment, where every feature serves welfare and beholding itself benefits. Form functions as blessing. Arrange home, altar, and attire with simple cleanliness and grace before prayer, inviting auspicious currents to gather and remain.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-172"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-934", title: "Viśvamātā", category: "Dharma-tattva", summary: "She who is the mother of the universe. Viśva-mātā means Mother of all, whose body is the cosmos and whose pervasion fulfills the Gītā's vision of Brahman enfolding everything. The passage returns to the opening name as Mother and even plays on viśva as a name of Viṣṇu, calling Her His mother too, underscoring that no deity stands outside Her maternity. All beings are Her children. In practice, regard strangers, creatures, and even opponents as siblings under one Mother, serving them as service to Her. The passage cites the teaching that Brahman stands pervading all, confirming omnipresence as Her exclusive mark and grounding the opening salute to the Mother. Motherhood is metaphysics. Practice seeing one stranger daily as Her child, offering silent goodwill that translates cosmic maternity into concrete kindness.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-173"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-935", title: "Jagaddhātrī", category: "Dharma-tattva", summary: "She who is the mother who protects and sustains the world. Jagad-dhātrī adds nursing support to maternity: She not only births but bears up, rules, and shelters all beings. The passage cites the Upaniṣadic Lord of all as ruler and protector, identifying that sustaining office with Hers. Providence is maternal. In practice, thank Her for daily maintenance of life, and imitate Her by reliably supporting those entrusted to your care. The passage invokes the Upanishadic ruler and protector of beings to define Her office of maintenance, continuing the creator's work through nurture. Sustenance is sacred. Sustain something fragile today, a person, animal, plant, or promise, with the steady reliability that mirrors Her world-bearing care.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-173"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-936", title: "Viśālākṣī", category: "Dharma-tattva", summary: "She who has large eyes. Viśāla-akṣī praises wide, compassionate, lustrous eyes celebrated in Saundarya Laharī and throughout this hymn. The passage observes that Lalitā lacks the usual boon-granting and fear-removing hands because She performs both offices through glances: vast eyes signify grace capacious enough to cover the universe at once. Seeing is saving. In practice, meet Her gaze in visualization when afraid or petitioning, trusting that to be seen by those eyes is already to be protected and blessed. The passage counts multiple earlier praises of those eyes and recalls hymns devoted entirely to their compassion, teaching that largeness signifies all-embracing scope and that beauty here is functional grace. Vision rescues. When interceding for others, picture them held within those wide eyes, trusting that encompassing gaze to supply what argument cannot.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-173"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-937", title: "Virāgiṇī", category: "Dharma-tattva", summary: "She who is dispassionate. Virāgiṇī means free from clinging passion, established in desirelessness already praised as absence of craving. The passage notes deliberate repetition in the hymn: such reiteration fixes cardinal truths, here that the Mother of all remains unbound by what binds Her children. Detachment grounds compassion. In practice, enjoy rightfully what comes without grasping, rehearsing Her freedom in small renunciations until equanimity steadies. The passage observes intentional repetition across the hymn to fix liberating truths, paralleling many affirmations of Her Brahmanhood, so that dispassion is learned by refrain. Rehearsal forms character. Repeat a brief renunciatory resolve when tempted by excess, letting Her freedom echo until craving loosens its grip.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-173"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-938", title: "Pragalbhā", category: "Dharma-tattva", summary: "She who is skillful and confident. Pragalbhā means bold, assured, resolute in conduct. The passage grounds this confidence in governance by karma's unfailing law: She never oversteps Her own ordinance, so Her assurance is righteousness itself rather than caprice. Order is Her courage. In practice, act resolutely within dharma once discerned, refusing both timidity and transgression, and trust lawful consequences to ripen. The passage grounds Her boldness in fidelity to karma's law, never transgressing the ordinance by which worlds are governed, so confidence and conscience coincide. Assurance serves order. Before bold action, verify its lawfulness and kindness; then proceed without apology, leaving results to the justice She administers.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-173"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-939", title: "Paramodārā", category: "Dharma-tattva", summary: "She who is supremely generous. Parama-udārā means highest nobility that gives unasked and beyond asking. Read as parā-modā-ā-ra, the name also means She grants all-encompassing supreme bliss, the threshold of liberation. The passage thus joins material liberality with spiritual bestowal, beyond time and space. Giving is Her nature. In practice, give cheerfully beyond strict obligation, and pray not only for goods but for the bliss that completes them. The passage parses supreme bliss given all around, treats that bliss as the threshold of final freedom, and even notes traditional lore linking planetary houses to release, all to magnify Her liberality. Generosity liberates. Give something beyond convenience this week, anonymously if possible, and notice how release of grip foretastes release of soul.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-173"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-940", title: "Parāmodā", category: "Dharma-tattva", summary: "She who is supremely joyful. Parā-modā means embodiment of highest delight, complementing the previous name: there She bestows bliss, here She is bliss. The passage teaches that vivid contemplation of Her beautifully described form itself awakens joy, since auspiciousness, grace, and compassion concentrated in one vision naturally flood the heart. Vision becomes rapture. In practice, dwell lovingly on each detail of Her form until description turns to delight and delight to stillness. The passage urges understanding each name to unlock the visualized splendor that spontaneously yields delight, since grace, compassion, and beauty concentrated in one form naturally overflow as joy. Comprehension flowers into felicity. Study one name deeply, picture its meaning, and rest in the gladness that understanding awakens.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-173"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-941", title: "Manomayī", category: "Dharma-tattva", summary: "She who is in the form of the mind. Mano-mayī means constituted of mental substance, able to transmute restless mind into divine consciousness. The passage cites yogic psychology on mind's restlessness and its cure through practice and dispassion, then describes perpetual recognition in which Śakti-Śiva union is felt in every act, not only in formal meditation, so that Brahman realized through mind transforms the mind itself. She is agent of that alchemy. In practice, fix the fidgeting mind repeatedly on the divine, extending remembrance from cushion into chores until all action feels witnessed. The passage invokes unobstructed sky-faring awareness born when union of powers is recognized continuously, transmuting mundane mind into divine consciousness where the still Lord shines revealed. Transformation exceeds meditation sessions. Dedicate transitions between tasks to brief remembrance, stitching scattered hours into one continuous contemplation.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-173"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-942", title: "Vyomakeśī", category: "Dharma-tattva", summary: "She who has the sky as Her hair. Vyoma-keśī means heaven or ether for tresses, matching Śiva as sky-haired Lord. The passage reads this as Her cosmic body: hair as atmosphere within the waking universal form, where the empirical world is Brahman's first vast embodiment. Immensity becomes adornment. In practice, look at the open sky as Her hair spread above you, feeling small worries loosen within that expanse. The passage situates this image in the waking cosmic body, first embodiment of Brahman as empirical universe, where atmosphere becomes ornament and vastness becomes intimacy. Grandeur adorns. Step outdoors at dusk, feel wind and space as Her tresses stirring, and let personal preoccupations thin into wonder.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-174"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-943", title: "Vimānasthā", category: "Dharma-tattva", summary: "She who is seated in Her celestial chariot; She who journeys in Her celestial chariot along with the gods. Vimāna can mean a divine vehicle, while vi-māna-sthā can also mean protectively established or dwelling as the immeasurable Brahman that limits itself into forms. The passage moves from chariots of war to Her occupation with sheltering all creatures, Her adoration by scriptures, and Her power to manifest the unconditioned in gracious measure. Mobility serves mercy. In practice, imagine Her swiftly arriving wherever called, and make yourself a fit landing-place through readiness and prayer. The passage derives protection from the syllables of accord and shelter, honors Her war and festival vehicles, and celebrates scriptural adoration of the immeasurable willing to be measured for love. Vastness visits. Before travel or undertaking, invoke Her vehicular presence, asking safe passage outward and steady abiding inward.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-174"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-944", title: "Vajriṇī", category: "Dharma-tattva", summary: "She who bears the vajrā (thunderbolt) weapon. Vajriṇī means wielder of the diamond-hard bolt, like Indra's weapon forged for cosmic defense and also suggestive of gem-like splendor. The passage hears the Upaniṣadic Brahman as a raised thunderbolt that awes sun, fire, wind, and death into duty, so Her bolt is discipline sustaining the universe and striking delinquency. Awe preserves order. In practice, welcome corrective shocks as Her thunderbolt recalling you to duty, and promptly repair neglected obligations. The passage retells the thunderbolt forged from seer's bones and the terror by which cosmic functionaries keep their posts, extending the emblem to Buddhist wisdom goddesses who wield the same diamond clarity. Severity safeguards. Inspect neglected duties toward family, work, and nature, correcting one lapse promptly as homage to Her disciplining bolt.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-174"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-945", title: "Vāmakeśvarī", category: "Dharma-tattva", summary: "She who is the presiding deity of the vamakeshvara tantra. Vāmakeśvarī here names the Goddess of the Vāmakeśvara Tantra, supreme scripture of Śrīvidyā's inner worship, distinct from an earlier similarly sounding epithet. The passage sketches its secrecy around syllables, its story of Śiva revealing the sixteenfold wisdom to Śakti, its Goddess as source of the universe, and Her reverse-face power projecting difference out of unity for left-hand practitioners. Esotericism guards intimacy. In practice, approach Tantric wisdom only under qualified guidance, prizing inward worship over outward display. The passage recounts the Goddess questioning the Lord about the sixteenfold wisdom beyond sixty-four treatises, His unveiling of hidden syllables joining fire, love, and point into potent formulas, and the reverse awareness projecting difference from unity. Mystery protects maturation. Keep advanced practices confidential under guidance, letting secrecy ripen power rather than pride.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-174"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-946", title: "Pañcayajñapriyā", category: "Dharma-tattva", summary: "She who is fond of the five forms of sacrifices. Pañca-yajña-priyā means lover of fivefold offering, whether the Vedic rites of fire, calendar, season, life, and soma, or the remembered five of honoring gods, sages, ancestors, creatures, and guests, or Viṣṇu's five temple services, or the five cosmic oblations culminating in human birth. The passage surveys these systems to show sacrifice as gratitude sustaining worlds. Worship completes ecology. In practice, practice one daily fivefold round: pray, study, remember forebears, feed creatures, and welcome guests as offerings to Her. The passage distinguishes heard revelation from remembered tradition, catalogs Vedic and domestic fives, temple approaches, and cosmic oblations from waters to embryo, presenting all as Her delight. Offering sustains being. Audit your week for the five kindnesses of worship, learning, ancestry, creatures, and guests, strengthening whichever has grown thin.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-174"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-947", title: "Pañcapretamañcādhiśāyinī", category: "Dharma-tattva", summary: "She who reclines on a couch made of the five corpses. Pañca-preta-mañca means a seat whose four legs are Brahmā, Viṣṇu, Rudra, and Mahādeva with Sadāśiva as the plank. The passage offers two readings: philosophically, these great functionaries are Her instruments for projection, maintenance, and resorption; energetically, without Her kinetic power even mighty stillness lies corpse-like, for Śiva stirs only united with Śakti. Supremacy is aliveness. In practice, rely on no office or talent apart from the living current, invoking Her energy before undertaking great responsibilities. The passage cites the wave-ocean verse on Her throne, correlates conception with mind and perception with senses, and invokes Tantric testimony that subtle stillness is known only through union with power, all to confirm Her Brahmanhood decisively. Stillness needs stirring. Before exercising authority, pray for animating wisdom, remembering office without spirit is corpse and spirit without office is drift.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-174"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-948", title: "Pañcamī", category: "Dharma-tattva", summary: "She who is the fifth (after brahmā, viṣṇu, rudra and Ishvara). Pañcamī means the Fifth, beyond the four functional divinities, identified with Sadāśiva's consort and with Vārāhī, fifth among Mothers. The passage also hears the fifth oblation that yields the embryo, and the fifth stage of nearness to God, utter isolation in freedom beyond dwelling-near, likeness, proximity, and union. Five completes ascent. In practice, aim beyond partial attainments toward complete release, dedicating each lesser success upward as a step, not a seat. The passage links the fifth rank to recreation after dissolution, where I-awareness outweighs this-awareness, and to graded proximities culminating in utter solitude of freedom. Last exceeds near. Refuse to idolize penultimate spiritual consolations, offering each attainment upward as fuel for the final complete release She grants.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-175"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-949", title: "Pañcabhūteśī", category: "Dharma-tattva", summary: "She who is the goddess of the five elements. Pañca-bhūta-īśī means sovereign of space, air, fire, water, and earth, which the Taittirīya traces successively from Brahman. The passage therefore calls Her both ruler and substance of elements, citing the Pañcadaśī teaching that the nondual is known by discerning it from these coverings, and noting Viṣṇu's five-gem garland of victory as kin to Her elemental mastery. Matter obeys Mother. In practice, honor the elements in body and world as Her extensions, using them temperately and gratefully. The passage traces truth, knowledge, and infinity unfolding into elements, commends discerning the nondual from its elemental veils, and garlands victorious Viṣṇu with five gems mirroring those elements, affirming Her non-difference from Him. Elements encode essence. Handle food, water, fire, air, and space reverently today, noticing Brahman shimmering through material masks.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-175"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-950", title: "Pañcasaṅkhyopacāriṇī", category: "Dharma-tattva", summary: "She who is worshipped using five objects (fragrance, flower, incense, lamp and food) of worship. Pañca-saṅkhyā-upacāriṇī means attended with fivefold reverence: scent, blossom, smoke, light, and nourishment. Each offering answers an element, earth, ether, air, fire, and water, establishing communion between worshiper and worshiped. The passage hears in this a thanksgiving for creation itself, since existence emerging through five elements is repaid with fivefold love. Ritual rehearses cosmology. In practice, offer the five attentively and simply each day, feeling each sense-gift return creation's generosity. The passage quotes the teaching that existence emerged from non-existence through elements, so fivefold worship repays the gift of embodiment with grateful recognition. Gratitude completes creation. Perform the five offerings slowly, naming the element each honors, and feel communication open between small self and sustaining Whole.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-175"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-951", title: "Śāśvatī", category: "Dharma-tattva", summary: "She who is eternal. Śāśvatī means perpetually enduring, ever again worshiped because ever present. The passage cites the Kaṭha vision of the one cosmic Self controlling all yet dwelling innermost, manifold in appearance while single in truth, whose seers alone find lasting happiness. Eternity is intimacy. In practice, repeat short remembrances through the day, anchoring fleeting hours to the One who does not pass. The passage renders the teaching of the single inner ruler manifold in manifestation, promising enduring happiness only to seers of that Self within. Frequency of worship answers eternity of presence. Bind remembrance to routine thresholds, doorways, meals, and lamps, so the timeless punctuates time.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-175"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-952", title: "Śāśvataiśvaryā", category: "Dharma-tattva", summary: "She who holds eternal sovereignty. Śāśvata-aiśvaryā joins permanence with dominion, whose true wealth is imperishable knowledge and welfare rather than hoarded goods. The passage treats this as the secure fortune no time erodes. Her reign does not alternate. In practice, invest chiefly in undecaying riches of learning, virtue, and devotion, holding outer prosperity as Her loan. The passage glosses permanent wealth as abundance of insight and prosperity that survives decay, fortune no thief or season can steal. True assets appreciate inwardly. Review what you call wealth, shifting effort from accumulation toward understanding and generosity that death cannot confiscate. Rest in Her permanence when outer fortunes shake and shift.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-175"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-953", title: "Śarmadā", category: "Dharma-tattva", summary: "She who is the giver of happiness. Śarman means shelter, comfort, and bliss, and dā means giver. The passage distinguishes Her gift from fleeting material pleasure: earlier names praise comforts for body and mind, but here the emphasis falls on enduring joy culminating in liberation. Relief ripens into release. In practice, receive pleasant things thankfully without mistaking them for the goal, and ask Her progressively for the happiness that does not end. The passage distinguishes an earlier name of comfort-giving from this deeper felicity, assigning one to bodily relief and another to mental beatitude, both culminating in liberating joy. Happiness has degrees. When comfortable, pause to ask for the next depth, that ease may mature from sensation into serenity and finally into freedom.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-175"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-954", title: "Śambhumohinī", category: "Dharma-tattva", summary: "She who deludes shiva. Śambhu-mohinī pictures Her enchanting even the sense-conquered Auspicious One, echoing Her sport of love that stirs stillness itself. The passage holds the paradox lightly: the Ascetic is moved only by Her, proving delight and detachment compatible at the summit. Enchantment is enlightenment's play. In practice, let holy beauty soften austerity, allowing love to make discipline tender rather than rigid. The passage recalls the wave of love's play that stirs even perfect self-mastery, proving Her charm moves the unmoved without diminishing His stature. Allurement adorns asceticism. Invite beauty into discipline, music into study, tenderness into resolve, trusting Her to enchant rigidity into living devotion.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-175"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-955", title: "Dharā", category: "Śāśvata-mudrā", summary: "She who is mother earth. Dharā means bearer, sustainer, wearer, keeper, and observer, gathering every act of support into earth herself. The passage identifies Her with prakṛti, the sustaining ground, noting earth's seed-syllable as a sign of that material creativity. To stand is to be held by Her. In practice, tread, till, and build mindfully, honoring soil, food, and dwelling as Her patient body. The passage enumerates bearing, carrying, wearing, keeping, and observing as modes of sustenance, linking earth's seed-syllable to material creativity. Support is sacrament. Carry one burden for another today, however small, consciously participating in Her world-bearing office.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-176"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-956", title: "Dharasutā", category: "Śāśvata-mudrā", summary: "She who is the daughter of dhara (himavat). Dhara-sutā means daughter of the mountain, recalling Pārvatī born to Himavat, king of snows, forests, and steadfast heights. The passage treats mountain, wood, and earth together as Her paternal home, rooting transcendence in landscape. The high becomes kin. In practice, honor lineage, place, and parents as vessels of Her advent, and seek steadiness like mountains in devotion. The passage recalls the mountain-daughter story already sung, widening fatherhood to peaks, woods, and all that endures majestically. Landscape becomes lineage. Spend time among hills or trees with filial reverence, learning endurance and shelter from the family into which She was born.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-176"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-957", title: "Dhanyā", category: "Śāśvata-mudrā", summary: "She who possessses great wealth; She who is extremely blessed. Dhanyā means fortunate, opulent, and worthy of thanks. The passage turns the epithet toward the final hour, contrasting bewildered, angry, grateful, and lucid deaths, and praises the dhanya frame that fixes thought on scripture and compassion, and the still higher white clarity resting in great sentences like I am Brahman, echoing Kṛṣṇa's promise that whatever is remembered at departure shapes destiny. Blessedness is readiness. In practice, rehearse holy remembrance daily so that gratitude becomes reflex when it matters most. The passage maps four deathbed frames from craving through anger to grateful reflection and finally luminous identity, crowned by the promise that final thought shapes passage. Preparation decides arrival. Memorize one liberating sentence and one beloved form now, rehearsing them until they surface spontaneously in every crisis.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-176"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-958", title: "Dharmiṇī", category: "Śāśvata-mudrā", summary: "She who is righteous. Dharmiṇī means embodying dharma, the established order of law, custom, and sacred observance. The passage presents Her not merely as patron of morality but as its living shape. Rightness has a face. In practice, learn one rule of right conduct deeply, keep it without exception, and let that steadiness become worship. The passage defines the established order of statute, usage, and observance as Her very shape, so righteousness is not imposed upon Her but expressed by Her. Law lives. Keep customary courtesies and sacred observances carefully, recognizing small fidelities as participation in cosmic order. Let that steadiness quietly govern speech, choice, and daily habit.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-176"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-959", title: "Dharmavardhinī", category: "Śāśvata-mudrā", summary: "She who promotes righteousness. Dharma-vardhinī means She increases virtue in devotees, while vardha also suggests cutting away deceitful worldliness. The passage notes that meditation on Śiva's restraint, the sun's purity, and Her spousal devotion implants those qualities, and that only through Her is Śiva attained. Growth requires pruning. In practice, invite Her to enlarge one virtue and excise one self-deception this season, reviewing progress candidly. The passage observes that contemplation of restrained, radiant, and devoted exemplars transmits those virtues, while Her surgery on illusion clears sight for the still Lord. Example educates. Choose one exemplar of Her retinue, study a story of their virtue, and imitate one trait concretely this fortnight.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-176"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-960", title: "Lokātītā", category: "Śāśvata-mudrā", summary: "She who transcends the worlds. Loka-atītā means beyond all realms, from Indra's heaven to Brahmā's and Viṣṇu's abodes, understood also as stages of awareness. The passage enthrones Her with Śiva in the highest Kailāsa of pure consciousness above the void where ritual-bound souls still return, reserving nonreturn for meditators on Brahman. Altitude is inward. In practice, refuse to settle for shiny intermediate experiences in meditation, aspiring steadily toward the pure consciousness where no return is needed. The passage describes void-meditations, perishable elemental abodes where ritualists return, and three pure eternal seats beyond them for meditators who merge without rebirth. Elevation is purification. Simplify one entanglement binding you to lower aims, directing freed energy toward the highest seat of pure awareness.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-176"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-961", title: "Guṇātītā", category: "Śāśvata-mudrā", summary: "She who transcends the guṇās. Guṇa-atītā means beyond sattva's clarity, rajas's drive, and tamas's inertia, hence beyond root nature where those strands lie still. The passage identifies this as Her attributeless Brahman aspect, unmoved mover of all qualities. Freedom founds variety. In practice, watch which strand dominates mood and action, neither indulging nor repressing, but witnessing until the witness stands free. The passage recalls root nature where the three strands rest unagitated, teaching that She as their source necessarily surpasses them. Transcendence enables governance. When diagnosing inner imbalance, remember the witness of strands is already beyond them, and rest briefly as that witness before adjusting conduct.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-176"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-962", title: "Sarvātītā", category: "Śāśvata-mudrā", summary: "She who transcends everything. Sarva-atītā gathers the previous two transcendences: beyond worlds and beyond qualities means beyond all. The passage adds that She knows the universe and is knowable by it, while Śiva remains aloof unless approached through Her, making pure awareness and transformed qualities the twin instruments of realization. Total beyond means total access through Her. In practice, surrender every category of achievement and identity at Her feet, asking to know the Knower beyond all knowns. The passage emphasizes that realization matures amid clarified awareness and beyond the climb from dullness through passion to lucidity, with consciousness and qualities as chief instruments. Supremacy guides method. Offer both your clarity and confusion to Her, asking that every state become fuel for the ascent beyond states.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-176"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-963", title: "Śamātmikā", category: "Śāśvata-mudrā", summary: "She who is of the nature of peace and bliss. Śama-ātmikā means tranquillity won through meditation and release from illusion forms Her very self. The passage renders the Māṇḍūkya portrait of the fourth: neither inward nor outward knowing, beyond organ, thought, and sound, pure self-awareness where world-appearance ceases, one without second, auspicious and calm. Peace is person. In practice, shorten outer stimulation daily, lengthen silence, and let Her peace settle like sediment clearing water. The passage traces the word from Vedic felicity to later inward stillness, gathering happiness, fortune, and emancipation from illusion into one tranquil essence. Calm contains blessing. Lengthen exhalation and soften gaze in meditation, inviting Her stillness to settle like evening over ruffled waters.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-176"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-964", title: "Bandhūkakusumaprakhyā", category: "Śāśvata-mudrā", summary: "She who resembles the bandhūka flower in beauty and grace. Bandhūka-kusuma-prakhyā compares Her to the bright yellow-red blossom prized for tenderness and glow. The passage rests in sheer loveliness: sometimes doctrine is delight in color and softness, reminding that the Absolute also pleases the eye. Beauty consoles. In practice, place a fresh red flower before Her image and let its brightness teach the heart to bloom tenderly. The passage identifies the tree whose tender brightness supplies the simile, content to let a single flower teach theology. Simplicity sings. Keep one vivid blossom in view during prayer, learning from its transient glow how beauty can praise without words.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-177"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-965", title: "Bālā", category: "Śāśvata-mudrā", summary: "She who never forsakes the nature of a child. Bālā means the nine-year-old girl, also the maiden-form born as Lalitā's daughter and the first mantra of Śrīvidyā. The passage describes that youthful icon, red-clad, moon-crowned, three-eyed, sunrise-brilliant on red lotus with book, rosary, and gestures of assurance, noting traditional preliminaries for its fruition and the Upaniṣadic verse hailing Brahman as woman, man, boy, and girl at once. Innocence is power. In practice, approach Her with childlike directness, offering a simple three-syllable prayer with trust before complexity. The passage details traditional safeguards for the potent formula and universalizes childhood into a metaphysical rank where Brahman appears as girl as well as man and sage. Youth instructs age. Recover beginner's wonder before approaching difficult doctrine, asking questions plainly and receiving answers gratefully like a child.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-177"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-966", title: "Līlāvinodinī", category: "Śāśvata-mudrā", summary: "She who delights in Her sport. Līlā-vinodinī means She plays at creating, preserving, and dissolving with effortless facility. The passage cites law-texts and Brahma-sūtra that creation is pastime for Brahman, spontaneous and motiveless, while names and forms school ignorance toward the truth that all is Brahman, hiding and seeking through māyā, with fortune herself as playmate. Cosmos is game. In practice, meet success and reversal as moves in Her play, responding with skill and humor rather than possessiveness. The passage reasons that motiveless spontaneity befits perfection, while apparent hide-and-seek with souls through illusion educates ignorance toward the recognition that all is Brahman. Play has purpose. Meet today's surprise, pleasant or not, as a move in Her game designed to awaken clearer seeing.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-177"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-967", title: "Sumaṅgalī", category: "Śāśvata-mudrā", summary: "She who is eternally auspicious; She who never becomes a widow. Su-maṅgalī means supremely fortunate, the faithful spouse who is also Umā, goodness that drives away evil and causes welfare. The passage links Viṣṇu's praise of the auspicious of auspicious, dismisses fear of days and planets for those who trust Her, and offers a lotus-feet verse gathering all nine planets beneath Her soles as remedy for astral dread. Auspiciousness protects. In practice, when anxious about times and stars, lay worry at Her feet and proceed with duty confidently. The passage quotes the temple verse enthroning all planets at Her feet and repeats ancestral assurance that days and stars cannot harm the surrendered, since She commands the very powers astrology maps. Refuge outranks omen. Replace one superstitious hesitation with prayerful action, proceeding dutifully while leaving fruits to Her larger auspiciousness.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-177"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-968", title: "Sukhakarī", category: "Śāśvata-mudrā", summary: "She who gives happiness. Sukha-karī means maker of ease and joy, continuing a thread of names on comfort and felicity. The passage treats this as Her settled office: to gladden devotees in body and heart as prelude to deeper bliss. Joy is pedagogy. In practice, receive small happinesses as Her handiwork and share them promptly, multiplying joy by distribution. The passage links this office to earlier teachings on comfort and blessing, presenting happiness as Her habitual signature rather than occasional favor. Kindness is constant. Become Her instrument by easing one person's burden today without publicity, extending the happiness you receive.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-177"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-969", title: "Suveṣāḍhyā", category: "Śāśvata-mudrā", summary: "She who is very attractive in Her beautiful rich garments and ornaments. Su-veṣa-āḍhyā means richly and tastefully adorned, beauty as sign of inward auspiciousness. The passage connects splendid dress with the next name's portrait of gracious married dignity. Adornment announces blessing. In practice, keep worship space and person clean and graceful on holy days, letting outer order reflect inner reverence. The passage treats splendid attire as legible auspiciousness, prelude to the fuller portrait of gracious wedded dignity that follows. Beauty signals blessing. Dress the altar thoughtfully and yourself modestly for worship, understanding outer care as language of inner honor. Let outer grace continually remind the heart of inward devotion.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-177"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-970", title: "Suvāsinī", category: "Śāśvata-mudrā", summary: "She who is ever auspiciously married. Suvāsinī means a gracious wife dwelling with her husband, well-dressed and rich in virtue. The passage gathers Rāmāyaṇa teachings from Anasūyā, Kausalyā, and Sītā on wifely devotion, fidelity, decorum, and endurance, concluding that Lalitā embodies every such excellence while remaining the cosmic Mother. Fidelity hallows power. In practice, honor committed love in word and deed, supporting partners and households as shrines of steadfast care. The passage gathers bridal counsels on regarding the husband as highest good beyond wealth, manners, or circumstance, and on conduct, truthfulness, and elders' wisdom as a woman's fortress. Devotion dignifies dependence. Strengthen one committed relationship through attentive service and truthful speech, seeing fidelity as spiritual discipline.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-177"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-971", title: "Suvāsinyarcanaprītā", category: "Śāśvata-mudrā", summary: "She who is pleased by the worship performed by married women. Suvāsinī-arcana-prītā means She delights when honored by virtuous wives, who are themselves revered at the close of the nine-covering rite. The passage treats their worship as especially potent because chastity, endurance, and household sanctity concentrate spiritual force. Purity empowers prayer. In practice, invite and honor such worship, or if so situated, offer household duties themselves as liturgy with mantra and lamp. The passage honors such women at the culmination of elaborate worship, recognizing household sanctity as concentrated spiritual authority worthy of veneration. Home becomes temple. If you share a home, consecrate its kitchen and threshold with cleanliness, lamp, and kind words, inviting Her pleasure to dwell there.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-178"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-972", title: "Āśobhanā", category: "Śāśvata-mudrā", summary: "She who is always radiant. Ā-śobhanā intensifies beauty into extreme brilliance. The passage frames the hymn of beauty as sequel to the hymn of bliss: first forty-one verses of rapture, then fifty-nine of loveliness, suggesting joy flowers into splendor. Radiance follows realization. In practice, after meditation, carry its afterglow into countenance and speech, letting inner light beautify ordinary encounters. The passage divides the great hymn of beauty into bliss then loveliness, suggesting rapture naturally radiates as visible splendor. Joy beautifies. After prayer, carry deliberate brightness into the next encounter, greeting someone warmly so inner radiance completes its circuit outward. Abide in that brightness until the mind itself grows luminous.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-178"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-973", title: "Śuddhamānasā", category: "Śāśvata-mudrā", summary: "She who is of pure mind; one who purifies the mind of Her worshipers. Śuddha-mānasā means untainted by sense-clinging, karma, or latent impression, hence realizable only in concentrated clarity. The passage reasons from omnipresence and identity teachings that the practitioner's mind must mirror Brahman's purity to recognize itself as That, making Her spotlessness both model and means. Like knows Like. In practice, cleanse the mind through single-pointed remembrance, promptly releasing intruding cravings back into breath and mantra. The passage reasons from omnipresence and great sayings that practitioners must embody the purity they seek, since only clarified mind can recognize itself as the boundless. Likeness enables recognition. Purify one mental habit of gossip, grudge, or greed this month, making room for Her stainless image to appear.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-178"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-974", title: "Bindutarpaṇasantuṣṭā", category: "Śāśvata-mudrā", summary: "She who is pleased by offerings to the bindu (of śrīcakra). Bindu-tarpaṇa-santuṣṭā means gladdened by libations upon the central point, also called the all-bliss wheel and seat of union. The passage outlines solemn aspersion with consecrated water and flowers at the innermost triangle's heart during nine-covering worship, prepared strictly under a teacher's direction, warning against impure admixtures. Center deserves finest. In practice, offer the clearest attention at the center of your own awareness each morning, pouring gratitude where multiplicity converges. The passage details consecrated water, invoked presence, floral offering, and teacher-prescribed precision, cautioning against intoxicating admixtures of left-hand excess. Exactitude expresses love. Refine one ritual detail under guidance, performing it slowly and correctly as a love-letter to the Center.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-178"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-975", title: "Pūrvajā", category: "Śāśvata-mudrā", summary: "She who is ahead of everyone; first born. Pūrva-jā means existing before creation, since Śiva projects Her and She projects the worlds. The passage contrasts eternal Brahman without name and form with māyā's transient pageant of names and forms, explaining priority as ontological rather than temporal. Firstness is foundership. In practice, begin decisions by remembering the Source before instruments, asking what the Eternal would approve before consulting the urgent. The passage contrasts nameless formless ground with Maya's theater of names and forms, explaining Her seniority as causal rather than chronological. Priority means principality. Begin creative projects by acknowledging their ultimate source, dedicating outcomes before laboring over means.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-178"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-976", title: "Tripurāmbikā", category: "Śāśvata-mudrā", summary: "She who is the mother of the tripuras (three cities). Tripura-ambikā means Mother of triads, presiding over the eighth veil, the innermost triangle whose sides encode creation, maintenance, and dissolution, waking, dreaming, and sleep, and every triple rhythm. The passage names its guardians, cites Tantric praise of Her as unified supreme power indwelling the creator, preserver, and ruler, and shows all triads merging into the point where being, awareness, and bliss unite as attributeless Brahman. Threes resolve in One. In practice, gather each triad you notice, knower, knowing, known, into single witnessing presence. The passage names triangle guardians, cites Tantric praise of Her as unified power indwelling the cosmic triad, and shows all threes dissolving into the point of being-awareness-bliss. Multiplicity merges. When overwhelmed by competing triples of duties, gather them mentally into one point of presence before acting sequentially with calm.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-178"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-977", title: "Daśamudrāsamārādhyā", category: "Śāśvata-mudrā", summary: "She who is worshipped by ten mudras. Daśa-mudrā-samārādhyā means honored with ten finger-seals that embody deity-energies and seal secret exchange between practitioner and Goddess. The passage explains ritual grammar of gesture, invocation, and recitation, assigns nine seals to the nine veils with the womb-seal pervading all, and reserves the tenfold seal for adepts of the sixteen-syllable mantra as union of triads like teacher, pupil, and Goddess. Hands speak. In practice, learn gestures only from a competent teacher, performing them privately with mantra and reverent restraint. The passage surveys broader ritual families and five-limbed worship of inscription, gesture, repetition, offering, and hospitality, teaching that joined hands of stillness and energy generate auspicious force for mastering potent syllables. Form focuses force. Never display sacred gestures casually; practice them privately with clean hands and collected mind.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-179"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-978", title: "Tripurāśrīvaśaṅkarī", category: "Śāśvata-mudrā", summary: "She for whom tripurāśrī is under control. Tripurā-śrī-vaśaṅkarī identifies the fifth veil's sovereign, accomplisher of all aims, as held within Lalitā's sway. The passage lists veil-sovereigns in ascending order, teaches that names and forms dissolve into Self while worshiping that circle amid Viṣṇu's tenfold preserver powers, and praises the hymn's art of building expectancy toward the final revelation. Parts serve Whole. In practice, honor intermediate attainments gratefully without stopping at them, moving steadily toward the Mother they foreshadow. The passage counts veil-sovereigns in order and teaches that objects merge into Self during this circuit, strengthening teacher-disciple bonds while ten preserver powers secure auspicious passage. Progress is graded. Respect stages of growth in yourself and others, neither rushing beginners nor lingering where you have already ripened.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-179"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-979", title: "Jñānamudrā", category: "Śāśvata-mudrā", summary: "She who is in the form of the jñāna mudrā. Jñāna-mudrā is the fingertip union of forefinger and thumb signifying soul-Brahman oneness, famed as the silent teaching gesture of Dakṣiṇāmūrti to his seer-pupils. The passage also reads mudrā as passport and bliss-giver: She is both seal of knowledge and conveyance into it, granting joy through understanding. Gesture is gospel. In practice, form the gesture during meditation while contemplating I and Whole as one, letting the circle of fingers remind awareness to close its gap. The passage tells of the southern-faced teacher initiating seer-sons through this silent circle, and parses bliss-giving in its syllables to show knowledge conferring joy. Silence teaches. Hold the gesture lightly during breath-awareness, reflecting that the small self already touches the boundless like fingertip touching thumb.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-179"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-980", title: "Jñānagamyā", category: "Śāśvata-mudrā", summary: "She who is to be attained through the yoga of knowledge. Jñāna-gamyā means approachable only by wisdom, the crown where meditation and devotion merge into realization. The passage gathers Gītā assurances that knowledge-sacrifice exceeds material offering, that loving union invites the yoga of discernment, and that grace dwelling in the heart kindles wisdom's lamp, with sages describing Brahman as eternal knowing bliss beyond cause and proof. Love ripens into light. In practice, study, devote, and meditate as one path, letting affection steady inquiry until recognition dawns. The passage adds reflections on all-knowingness as cosmic rather than individual, and sage verses on the limitless sky-like Brahman beyond cause, proof, and modification, familiar as the ground of self-awareness. Wisdom widens. Test a flash of insight against conduct: if it increases compassion and steadiness, treasure it; if not, release it.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-179"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-981", title: "Jñānajñeyasvarūpiṇī", category: "Śāśvata-mudrā", summary: "She who is both knowledge and the known. Jñāna-jñeya-svarūpiṇī means She is at once the knowing light and the reality to be known, stressing wisdom once more at the hymn's close. The passage cites the Gītā's light of lights seated in every heart beyond illusion, defines the knowable as what must be sought and verified, and recalls the white-horse teaching that enjoyer, enjoyed, and inner director are all Brahman. Seeker and sought coincide. In practice, turn inquiry back upon the inquirer, asking who knows, until knowing itself shines as Her. The passage stresses repeated celebration of wisdom at the hymn's close, defining the knowable as duty to investigate and perceive, and affirming triad of enjoyer, enjoyed, and director as Brahman within. Inquiry is obligation. Pose one honest question about your true nature nightly, resting without forced answer until clarity surfaces.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-179"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-982", title: "Yonimudrā", category: "Śāśvata-mudrā", summary: "She who is in the form of the yonimudrā. Yoni-mudrā, ninth of the ten seals, emblems the divine procreative source through which the practitioner seeks authorization for rebirth into knowledge. The passage links Kṛṣṇa's great Brahman as womb of beings with Tantric praise of this seal for healing, mantra-success, self-vision, and liberation, distinguishing liturgical, sensory-withdrawal, and kuṇḍalinī forms, all to be kept private. Origin becomes initiation. In practice, guard such potent practices with secrecy and qualified guidance, using them to gestate understanding rather than display. The passage quotes the primordial womb of creatures and Tantric praise of healing, mantra-success, self-perception, and liberation, veiling technique in secrecy. Source empowers seeking. Approach procreative and spiritual mysteries with reverence and restraint, asking Her authorization before exploring depths that reshape identity.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-180"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-983", title: "Trikhaṇḍeśī", category: "Śāśvata-mudrā", summary: "She who is the ruler of the tenth mudrā, the trikhaṇḍa. Tri-khaṇḍa-īśī governs the invoking seal that fuses triads, pupil, teacher, Goddess, or knower, known, knowing, into single Brahman-awareness. The passage also hears the three mantra-portions and the triple cosmic offices of projection, maintenance, and resorption in its outstretched fingers. Threefold becomes onefold. In practice, at worship's start consciously unite yourself, your guide, and the Goddess in one intention before proceeding. The passage also hears the three mantra-portions in this seal and titles it broadly as the Brahma-gesture whose extended fingers signal projection, maintenance, and withdrawal. Invocation integrates. Use beginnings of worship to consciously gather scattered aims into one unified aspiration toward truth.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-180"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-984", title: "Triguṇā", category: "Śāśvata-mudrā", summary: "She who is endowed with the three guṇas of sattva, rajas and tamas. Tri-guṇā means embodying lucidity, passion, and inertia, the strands of nature distributing unevenly in every body and mind to shape thought and deed. The passage expounds Kṛṣṇa's analysis of their binding and liberating effects and resolves the apparent tension with attributelessness: without qualities She is the transcendent Brahman, with qualities She is the maternal Brahman from whom qualities arise to make creation possible. Strands are Hers. In practice, nourish lucidity, harness drive, and dispel dullness, offering all three back to their source. The passage details how uneven distribution shapes character, how lucidity binds through joy and wisdom, passion through acquisitive doing, and inertia through error and sloth, urging transcendence toward sorrowless bliss. Mixture explains temperament. Observe your dominant strand without self-contempt, then deliberately strengthen lucidity through study, cleanliness, and calm routine.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-180"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-985", title: "Ambā", category: "Śāśvata-mudrā", summary: "She who is mother of all beings; mother of the universe. Ambā is the ardent cry of Mother bursting from the hymn's seers as doctrine nears ecstasy. The passage suggests overwhelmed love interrupts taxonomy with direct address, while also glimpsing a linguistic teaching that every attributive utterance about Brahman finally resolves into indivisible reality, of which this tender name is cause and proof. Exclamation is exposition. In practice, repeat Mother softly with feeling until argument stills and only grateful intimacy remains. The passage introduces the linguistic idea of attributive expression whose content remains indivisible reality, making this cry of Mother the cause and seal of all descriptions. Love completes logic. When theology grows dry, interrupt analysis with heartfelt address, allowing affection to restore the living referent behind concepts.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-180"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-986", title: "Trikoṇagā", category: "Śāśvata-mudrā", summary: "She who resides in the triangle. Tri-koṇa-gā means dwelling in the innermost triangle granting all attainments, facing the worshiper. The passage unfolds creation-graphics: triangle as Śakti, point as Śiva, their pulsation birthing further triangles; three sides as will, knowing, doing, or waking worlds; pedestal and emblem united like Umā and Maheśvara; mental worship commended over display. Geometry is genesis. In practice, visualize the central triangle with its luminous point, breathing there quietly and privately as taught by your tradition. The passage elaborates throbbing divinity, triple powers and speeches and offices, triple fortresses of deities sustaining worlds, experimental triads of test, experience, and object, and pedestal-emblem union praised in lore. Symbol systematizes. Study the diagram only with reverent guidance, translating each line into an aspect of your own awareness.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-180"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-987", title: "Anaghā", category: "Śāśvata-mudrā", summary: "She who is sinless. An-aghā means free from fault, impurity, and suffering despite administering the three cosmic offices. The passage stresses unstained administration: overseeing merit and demerit never soils Her, as She remains Śiva's eternally pure extension. Governance leaves no stain. In practice, perform necessary judgment and correction without malice, asking Her to keep your hands clean while doing difficult duties. The passage insists that supervising the three exclusive offices of the Absolute never compromises Her, since She extends the eternally stainless stillness into activity without adhesion. Action need not stain. Perform obligatory corrections firmly yet without hatred, checking afterward that the heart remains clear and kind.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-180"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-988", title: "Adbhutacāritrā", category: "Śāśvata-mudrā", summary: "She whose deeds are marvelous. Adbhuta-cāritrā praises conduct wonderful and supernatural, while cāritra also evokes a woman's unsullied integrity. Following the denial of stain, the passage affirms stupendous purity and power in Her qualified Brahman form, perfectly placed to close negation with wonder. Purity astonishes. In practice, recount one marvelous turn of grace witnessed in life, letting amazement renew trust more than explanation could. The passage balances prior negation with affirmation, describing qualified Brahmanhood as both stainless and spectacularly gracious, with feminine integrity crowning cosmic performance. Wonder worships. Share an account of grace discreetly to strengthen another's faith, attributing goodness to Her rather than to chance.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-180"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-989", title: "Vāñchitārthapradāyinī", category: "Śāśvata-mudrā", summary: "She who gives all the desired objects. Vāñchita-artha-pradāyinī means granter of whatever devotees sincerely seek, already sung in earlier names and tripled in the three-hundred. The passage cites the verse where other gods grant by hand-gestures while Her feet themselves shelter the fearful and exceed their asking, hiding the maiden-mantra in its syllables, and adds a teacher's counsel to begin with feet, hymns, repetition, and meditation, praying to depart meditating on Her into unity and bliss. Feet outgive hands. In practice, place desires at Her feet daily, then meditate on those feet until wanting ripens into belonging. The passage triples the promise across the shorter hymn and preserves a teacher's ladder from reading to repetition to meditation on lotus feet, with the dying prayer to depart contemplating Her into total merger and bliss itself. Method matures stepwise. Establish that ladder now: read briefly, repeat sincerely, sit silently, and seal practice with prayer for final remembrance.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-180"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-990", title: "Abhyāsātiśayajñātā", category: "Śāśvata-mudrā", summary: "She who is known only through the exceedingly streneous practice of spiritual discipline. Abhyāsa-atiśaya-jñātā means realized through surpassing, sustained application of attention. The passage stresses mind-culture and inward worship, citing the Brahma-sūtra rule that liberating sentences must be heard, reflected, and meditated repeatedly, with Kṛṣṇa's promise to secure and carry those ceaselessly united to Him. Having displayed gross, subtle, and subtlest forms, the seers now prescribe seeing through practice. Repetition reveals. In practice, fix a modest daily meditation and keep it without break, letting constancy do what intensity alone cannot. The passage cites the aphorism on necessary repetition through hearing, reflection, and absorption in great identity-sentences, with the promise of secured welfare for the ceaselessly united. Having displayed every form, the seers prescribe practice as presence. Never abandon the seat; let perseverance achieve what brilliance cannot force.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-181"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-991", title: "Ṣaḍadhvātītarūpiṇī", category: "Śāśvata-mudrā", summary: "She whose form transcends the six paths. Ṣaḍ-adhva-atīta-rūpiṇī means beyond the six ways of worship: verse, abode-diagram, outer form or syllable, principle, ritual art or time, and mantra-sound. The passage divides the first three as expression belonging to Śakti's reflective power and the latter three as meaning belonging to Śiva's luminous ground, whose union births the world, so the adept climbs from sound-perfection through worlds, principles, and time to realize Her who exceeds them. Means are ladders. In practice, use image, text, and mantra faithfully while remembering they point beyond themselves to silent union. The passage maps sound-forms to reflective power and sense-forms to luminous ground, guiding the adept from mantra-mastery through worlds, principles, and time toward realization, and announces the coming canticle of union. Structure serves surrender. Honor each aid, image, syllable, and calendar, while holding lightly to all as ferryboats toward wordless meeting.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-181"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-992", title: "Avyājakaruṇāmūrtiḥ", category: "Śāśvata-mudrā", summary: "She who is pure compassion. Avyāja-karuṇā-mūrti means unfeigned mercy embodied, without pretense or partiality. The passage contrasts Śiva's disciplining rod with Śakti's soothing tenderness and recalls earlier hymns to Her pity and celebrated verses on compassionate eyes, condemning show-compassion by displaying the real. Motherhood is mercy. In practice, perform one hidden kindness expecting neither credit nor return, letting action mirror Her unpretending care. The passage recalls triple celebrations of Her pity and the hymn on compassionate glances, setting maternal tenderness against stern discipline to reveal completeness. Wrath corrects, mercy restores. Balance firmness with gentleness in guiding others, correcting error clearly yet embracing the erring warmly as She does.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-181"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-993", title: "Ajñānadhvāntadīpikā", category: "Śāśvata-mudrā", summary: "She who is the bright lamp that dispels the darkness of ignorance. Ajñāna-dhvānta-dīpikā means the lamp hose light is wisdom scattering beginningless spiritual darkness. The passage explains She radiates Śiva's self-luminosity as His autonomous reflective power that projects worlds, so removal of ignorance and dawn of realization follow swiftly like sunrise after night, echoing Kṛṣṇa's indwelling lamp in the heart. She analyzes ignorance as positive, indescribable, triple-stranded superimposition that knowledge alone dissolves. Light liberates. In practice, carry inquiry into one persistent confusion, praying for Her lamp until superimposition gives way to seeing. The passage defines ignorance as positive, triple-stranded, beginningless superimposition of world upon ground, neither real nor unreal yet potent, removable by knowing though its fruits linger like momentum. Diagnosis directs cure. Keep company with clear teachings and truthful friends, avoiding inputs that thicken superimposition while Her lamp does its work.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-181"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-994", title: "Ābālagopaviditā", category: "Śāśvata-mudrā", summary: "She who is known well by all, even by children and cowherds. Ā-bāla-gopa-viditā means recognized from child to cowherd, from simplest laborer to highest divinity. The passage reads cowherd broadly as absorbed workers without leisure for study, praising their consecrated action as a slower but valid road, and cites the ancient hymn where herders and water-bearers behold the Lord. She is not a scholars' monopoly. In practice, sanctify manual work with remembrance, doing each task attentively as offering while trusting gradual ripening. The passage suggests the phrase may span highest divinity to smallest child, citing the ancient hymn where laborers behold the Lord, and praises action-yoga as honored though slower than knowing. Simplicity qualifies. Welcome children, workers, and strangers into sacred conversation without condescension, recognizing Her knowledge dawning in every sincerity.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-182"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-995", title: "Sarvānullaṅghyaśāsanā", category: "Śāśvata-mudrā", summary: "She whose commands are not disobeyed by anyone. Sarva-anullaṅghya-śāsanā means even Brahmā, Viṣṇu, and Rudra follow Her ordinance in the five cosmic acts. The passage recalls the celebrated verse where creation, preservation, dissolution, concealment, and grace proceed at a mere movement of Her creeper-like brows, tracing re-creation's descent from stillness through successive powers back to Brahmā's new world. Command is cosmos. In practice, discern Her order in conscience and scripture, obeying promptly in small duties so that greater guidance finds you ready. The passage details the five offices from emergence through concealment to grace, their re-creative reversal from stillness back to new worlds, all approved by a brow's movement. Hierarchy obeys love. Align personal leadership with Her example: command clearly, gently, and accountably, seeking consent of conscience before demanding compliance of others.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-182"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-996", title: "Śrīcakrarājanilayā", category: "Śāśvata-mudrā", summary: "She who abides in śrīcakra, the king of chakras. Śrī-cakra-rāja-nilayā enthrones Her in the supreme diagram, body of Śiva and Śakti where every deity has place. The passage tours its gates from outer earth-square deluding triple awareness through sixteen and eight-petalled lotuses to layered triangles for blessing, accomplishment, protection, and healing, each with guardian and yoginī, culminating past the seer-goddesses and armories toward triangle and point. Diagram is pilgrimage. In practice, enter worship gradually from outer purification toward inner stillness, seeking permission of each level through humility before advancing. The passage enumerates enclosures, rulers, seers, petals, triangles, circles, squares, powers, and mothers with their permissions, stressing reverent sequential entry from outermost marvel to innermost stillness. Order protects intimacy. Do not rush toward depths; prepare body, breath, and ethics patiently so the King of Diagrams admits you graciously.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-182"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-997", title: "Śrīmattripurasundarī", category: "Śāśvata-mudrā", summary: "She who is the divine tripurasundarī devī. Śrīmat-tripura-sundarī hails the Beauty of the Three Fortresses, consort of Tripura who wields creation, maintenance, and dissolution through Brahmā, Viṣṇu, and Rudra as limbs of His body. The passage adds Tantric testimony of primordial light birthing worlds as mother of syllables, the triangle-guardians of those offices, and manifestation through will, knowing, and doing encoded in mantra-portions, smiling at modern cosmology's expansion and contraction as Her triple pulse. Threefold power wears loveliness. In practice, admire Her beauty as governance made gracious, trusting the same hands that create to sustain and renew you. The passage cites invocations of primordial light and maternal syllables birthing triple worlds, fortress-lines as cosmic offices, mantra-portions as powers of will, knowing, and doing, and modern expansion-contraction as echo of Her triple pulse. Antiquity anticipates discovery. Let scientific wonder deepen devotion, seeing measured cosmos as Her lovely body in motion.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-182"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-998", title: "Śrīśivā", category: "Non-dual seal", summary: "She who is the auspicious and divine shiva. Śrī-Śivā prefixes fortune's seed to the Auspicious One, proclaiming Her most benign form already praised simply as Śivā. Doctrinally this seal gathers liberation, purity beyond world-appearance, healing graciousness sung in Rudra hymns, and the blissful readiness to behold the coupled radiance in the next name before final merger. The coiled feminine current nears its meeting with the masculine stillness, crowning long discipline. The seers placed this shining threshold here so that paradoxical omnipresence becomes felt experience. In practice, visualize Her all-auspicious and healing, resting thought in goodness itself until bliss steadies into readiness for unity.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-183"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-999", title: "Śivaśaktaikyarūpiṇī", category: "Non-dual seal", summary: "She who is the union of shiva and śaktī into one form. Śiva-śakti-aikya-rūpiṇī is the hymn's most guarded mystery, the confluence creating, sustaining, dissolving, and renewing worlds under karma's law. Doctrinally still Parabrahman without desire meets primordial Parāśakti who manifests, like fire inseparable from heat or mirror needed for splendor to behold itself. The passage unfolds light and reflection, I and This, foundational and operative awareness, twin drops expanding into word and meaning, then a mingled drop forming the triangle-heart of the sacred diagram. Two are One. In practice, meditate on breath, knowing, and act as Their embrace, refusing to split stillness from energy in yourself.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-183"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-1000", title: "Lalitāmbikā", category: "Non-dual seal", summary: "She who is the divine mother lalitā. Lalitāmbikā at last utters Her intimate name, meaning playful, tender, charming, lovely, desired, and gently tremulous, every sense fitting Her. Doctrinally this seal gathers the whole hymn: Mother of the first five names encoding five cosmic acts, source of energies, Vedas, syllables, and gods, compassionate yet just, reached through ritual, mantra, and visualization culminating in pure knowing where mind becomes Brahman. Play crowns metaphysics, for the One beyond grasp chooses to be gracious and knowable as Mother. The seers lay these thousand lights at Her feet for universal welfare. In practice, complete study by resting in silent loving surrender at Her feet, letting play, grace, and awareness merge into endless bliss.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-183"] },
];
